From 68087d16822d862e6f32d0643150b697ba245ac7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Golle <daniel@makrotopia.org>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 13:18:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] media: rockchip: Add the RV1126 ISPP driver

The RV1126 SoC pairs the ISP2 with an image post-processing unit
(ISPP) that applies temporal noise reduction, noise reduction,
sharpening and scaling to the YUV422 stream coming out of the ISP.

The driver matches the rockchip,rv1126-ispp device and registers the
rkispp-subdev entity with input, output, params and stats pads, one
video device per output stream (the module bypass and the three
scalers), the II input stream, the params and stats devices carrying
the v4l2_isp_buffer blocks, and a debugfs node. The module bypass
output offers NV12 and NV12M, the scalers derive their chroma base from
the luma base and stay single plane.

The pipeline input is the ISP output pool handed over through the ISP
pipe subdev, or the II stream frames written back from DDR when the
input port is not connected. The engine is soft reset after every frame
and every module register is restored from the last written value, as
the register readback does not return it. The SMMU sits inside the
ISPP register window and loses its attachment in that reset, so the
reset re-attaches the iommu domain; the attach sleeps, which is why the
interrupts are handled in a thread. The queued params blocks apply per
module at the module frame ends, a restart re-applies the current
state of the enabled modules, and a monitor work restarts the pipeline
when a module stops completing frames.

A scaler stopped while the pipeline keeps running finishes the frame
it is writing: its disable takes effect at the frame boundary and its
frame end interrupt stays away, so the frame completion gate leaves a
stopping scaler out and the stream releases its buffers once the
per-frame reset that follows the frame has run. The last stream stop
cancels the interrupt and monitor works and soft resets the
engine before the internal buffers are freed, so neither a work nor a
writer still in flight reaches freed memory; the device removal
cancels the works as well.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Daniel Golle <daniel@makrotopia.org>
